spec.section:
### I.3.1 Worked example: grant-use without use (D-009)

`mgr-comms` has:

```json
{
  "permissions": {
    "use": {"capabilities": [], "resource_scope": []},
    "grant": {
      "capabilities": ["telegram.send"],
      "resource_scope": ["telegram:delivery-gate-17"],
      "recipients": {"agents": ["sup-crier"], "roles": [], "tiers": ["supervisor"]}
    },
    "confer_delegation": {"capabilities": [], "resource_scope": []}
  }
}
```

The director first verifies the VP pool and approval condition, then `mgr-comms` calls
`grant_issue` for `telegram.send` to `sup-crier`. The call succeeds because R2 checks
`grant_use`, not `use`; the response includes `grant_id`, `origin: director_grant`,
`issuer: mgr-comms`, `recipient: sup-crier`, scope, policy revision and a readback reference.
The supervisor's own approval policy is evaluated when `sup-crier` calls `telegram.send`.
If that policy requires approval, the call returns its normal pending-approval result until the
configured approver resolves it; the grant does not bypass the policy.

When `mgr-comms` attempts `telegram.send` itself, R1 finds no `use` capability and returns
`403 tool_not_granted`. The service writes one audit record containing the actor, operation,
resource, `decision: deny`, `cause: tool_not_granted`, and the relevant policy revision. The
successful issuance and failed self-use are independent facts.
